.TH XYMONAPI-RAG 1 "October 30, 2026" "xymon-api"
.SH NAME
xymonapi-rag \- search Xymon's manual pages and history by meaning
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "rag/xymonapi-rag.sh index [--no-history] [--full]"
.br
.B "rag/xymonapi-rag.sh search [--kind docs|history] [-k N] QUESTION"
.br
.B "rag/xymonapi-rag.sh ask [-k N] QUESTION"
.br
.B "rag/xymonapi-rag.sh eval [-k N] [--min HITS] FILE"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi-rag finds the passages of Xymon's manual pages, and of its history,
that a question is about, by what they mean rather than by the words they
share.
.IP "\fBindex\fR"
Reads the manual pages under $XYMONAPI_RAG_DOCS and, through
.I xymonapi(8),
the colour changes since $XYMONAPI_RAG_SINCE with the status text saved at
each -- page by page, the newest $XYMONAPI_RAG_MAX_HISTORY of them at most;
cuts them into passages, one per manual-page
section or item and one per change; and stores a vector for each in
$XYMONAPI_RAG_INDEX. A passage the index holds already keeps its vector, and
a change it holds has its text not fetched again, so a run that adds a
day's changes takes seconds; \fB--full\fR embeds everything again. Run it
to take in what has changed: the task \fBtasks.d/xymonapi-rag\fR, which
make install writes DISABLED, does so once a day, and
.I xymonapi(8)
reads the new index at once. To run it, remove that line and put
XYMONAPI_RAG_DOCS -- and XYMONAPI_USER and XYMONAPI_PASSWORD when the API
asks for them -- in a file under xymonserver.d/ that only the Xymon user
can read. When
the history cannot be read -- a /histlog refused for another reason than a
status that was not saved -- it stops and leaves the index as it was. An
API that does not answer yet, as at start-up when the task runs before
xymonapi listens, is tried again for about a minute first.
\fB--no-history\fR reads the manual pages only.
.IP "\fBsearch\fR"
Prints, as JSON, the N passages nearest QUESTION (5 by default, 20 at most),
each with its kind, the page, section and item or the host, test and time it
comes from, and its score. \fB--kind\fR keeps to manual pages or history.
Where there is no index -- a laptop -- it asks the API's /search instead,
which reads the index on the Xymon server: then neither the manual pages
nor the embedding model are needed here.
.IP "\fBask\fR"
Gives the N passages nearest QUESTION, found as search finds them, and the
question, to a language model that speaks the OpenAI-compatible API, and
prints its answer.
.IP "\fBeval\fR"
Asks every question of FILE, a JSON list of objects with a "question", the
"kind" to search and the text its "expect"ed passage's title holds, and
prints one line per question and the score: how many found their passage
among the first N (3 by default). With \fB--min\fR it exits 1 below that
many hits, for a test. tests/eval/docs.json is such a file for Xymon's own
manual pages.
.LP
The vectors come from a small embedding model, run on this machine by the
Python module fastembed; it is downloaded once, about 65 MB, the first time
it is used. Indexing and searching send nothing elsewhere. Only ask does,
and only to $XYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_URL.

The same search is offered to an AI client by the search_docs and
search_history tools of
.I xymonapi-mcp(1),
whose own model then writes the answer: with a Claude subscription, ask is
not needed.

It needs Python 3.10 or later, and, to index or to search an index here,
the modules in rag/requirements.txt; when one is missing it says so and
exits with status 77. \fBmake install-client\fR puts it under a PREFIX of
yours, as the command xymonapi-rag.

.SH SECURITY
A history passage holds a status text, which the monitored host wrote: a
host that is broken into can write there words meant to steer a language
model. Each such passage carries a "trust" field saying it is data, not
instructions, and ask tells the model so; a model may still be misled. Give
ask, and an AI client that uses search_history, no more than read access,
as the tools of xymonapi-mcp(1) have.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_DOCS\fR"
A directory searched for manual pages: files ending in .1, .5, .7 or .8,
gzipped or not, as in a Xymon source tree or under /usr/share/man. Only
Xymon's pages are indexed -- those whose .TH line names Xymon -- so other
programs' pages, and any other file there, are left out.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_INDEX\fR"
The index file. The default is ~/.cache/xymonapi-rag/index.npz.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_SINCE\fR"
How far back index reads the history, as a time /events takes: -30d by
default.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_MAX_HISTORY\fR"
How many changes index takes at most, the newest first: 5000 by default.
Each is a passage to embed, and a server where thousands of changes happen
in a month would otherwise take long to index.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_OFFLINE\fR"
When set to anything but 0 or no, the embedding model is taken from
fastembed's cache and never downloaded: for a server without a way to
huggingface.co. Put the model there first, by running index or search once
on a machine that has one and copying the cache -- $FASTEMBED_CACHE_PATH,
or the system's temporary directory -- or by setting FASTEMBED_CACHE_PATH
to a directory seeded that way.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_MODEL\fR"
The embedding model, BAAI/bge-small-en-v1.5 by default. The index records
the model that built it, and a search with another is refused: run index
again after changing it.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_URL\fR"
The OpenAI-compatible API ask uses, an http:// or https:// URL. The default,
http://127.0.0.1:11434/v1, is a local Ollama: no key, no cost. A redirect
from it is an error, not followed: the key below would go to the host it
names.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_MODEL\fR"
The model ask names, llama3.1 by default. A server that has not got it
answers 404, and ask says so, naming the model: with Ollama, \fBollama pull
\fIMODEL\fR gets it.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_KEY\fR"
Sent as a bearer token when set, for a hosted model that needs a key.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_MAX_TOKENS\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_RAG_LLM_TIMEOUT\fR"
How many tokens the model may answer with, 2048 by default, and how many
seconds ask waits for it, 300. A reasoning model counts its thinking in the
tokens, and may spend them all on it: ask then says so. A plain model, such
as llama3.2, is the better fit.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_URL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TOKEN_FILE\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_USER\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_PASSWORD\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_PASSWORD_FILE\fR"
Where the API is, and the token, or the user, to send it, as for
xymonapi-mcp(1). As an
xymonlaunch task, index reads these from a file under xymonserver.d/,
which every Xymon program and CGI loads: put the password in a file of its
own that the Xymon user alone can read, and name it in
XYMONAPI_PASSWORD_FILE, so that the environment holds only its name.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
The Python to run with. The default is python3, found on PATH.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonapi(8), xymonapi-mcp(1)
